{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Then both of them shall die</b> — to exclude one who performs an act of titillation (חִדּוּדִים) from which the woman derives no pleasure. \"Also\" (גַּם), to include those who come after them — this is the language of Rabbenu Shelomo. But I do not understand it: is she a virgin, that we should need either to exclude or to include those who come after them? What difference is it to me whether first, second, or third? And furthermore, he should have said \"those who come after him.\" And in the versions that we have of the Sifre (Ki Teitzei 102): \"both of them\" — and not one who performs an act of titillation; since it says \"also both of them,\" to include those who come after them — and this is intercourse not in her natural manner, and Scripture includes it here, and all the forbidden unions derive from one another. And according to the plain sense: \"also both of them\" — the man is more deliberate in the sin, for he is the one who initiates, entices, and performs the act, and also the woman. And this verse mentions and specifies \"also both of them\": the man who lies with her and also the woman. And so it is the way of Scripture to attribute the sin to the man, as I explained (Leviticus 20:17) concerning \"he has uncovered his brother's nakedness; they shall be childless.\" And there are those who say that the reason for \"the man who lies with the woman\" and \"and the woman\" is an appositional addition (תּוֹסֶפֶת בֵּאוּר), like \"he shall bring it, the terumah of YHWH\" (Exodus 35:5), or \"that Nebuchadnezzar\" (Jeremiah 27:8). And in the Sifre (there): \"the man who lies with the woman\" — even if she is a minor; \"and the woman\" — even if she was had by a minor. If so, the reason for \"also both of them\" is: even each one of them, the man or the woman, whichever of them is subject to liability."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The citation of the Sifre is attributed to 